, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Fixed: `round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`. Before the change it returned `2.67`, the same as Python's built-in `round(2.675, 2)`.\n\n**Cause:** the float `2.675` is actually stored as `2.67499999999999982\u2026`, so `round()` was right to go down. `round()` also sends exact halves to the nearest even digit (`0.125` \u2192 `0.12`), which doesn't match the \"half up\" promised in the docstring.\n\n**Fix** (`pricing/money.py`): `round_money` now turns the number into a `Decimal` from its shortest printed form (`2.675`), rounds to cents with `ROUND_HALF_UP`, and still returns a `float`, so callers like `add_vat` don't change. Negative halves now round away from zero (`-2.675` \u2192 `-2.68`).\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_round_money.py`, covering the reported case, exact halves (`0.125` \u2192 `0.13`, `1.005` \u2192 `1.01`), values just either side of a half, an integer, a negative half, and `add_vat`. pytest isn't installed here, so I ran the same checks plus the existing `test_split` case as plain assertions with `python3`, and they all passed. The pytest file itself hasn't been run.\n\nOne case this doesn't cover: if a calculation like `x * 1.2` lands a tiny bit below a true half (e.g. `\u20260.00499999`), it will still round down. Removing that fully would mean doing the prices in `Decimal` from the start, not just at the rounding step.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 2, "replies_ok": 9, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 248868, "requests_cache_write": 10113, "requests_cost_usd": 0.1537986, "requests_count": 9, "requests_input_uncached": 20, "requests_output": 2669, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "omp:trig-omp-bugfix-regress:claude-opus-5-5:trial25", "run_seed": 20261014, "sampling_observed": null, "schedu